  <h2 id='repetition'>14.2 Repetition over dependency</h2>
  <p>
    The instinct that produces drilling is a good one:
    <em>don't fetch the same thing twice</em>. For most of React's history, the only way to honor it
    was to fetch once at the top and share downward. Hooks and context were the first crack in that
    constraint; in Next, deduped fetches and cached functions remove it entirely.
    <strong
      >It is better for two components to ask the same question independently than to be wired
      together</strong
    >, because the framework guarantees the question is only answered once per request.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' />
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Parent as data router'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[id]/page.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export default async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">Page</span>({ params }) {
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> { id } = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> params;
  <span class="tok-com">// This fetches the union of every child's needs.</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span>(id);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;&gt;
      &lt;<span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span>
        <span class="tok-prop">name</span>={program.name}
        <span class="tok-prop">status</span>={program.status}
        <span class="tok-prop">sponsor</span>={program.sponsor} /&gt;
