          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">components/program/ProgramHeader.tsx</span><span class="tok-kw">export async function</span> <span class="tok-fn">ProgramHeader</span>({ programId }) {
  <span class="tok-com">/* It asks for itself; the dedupe layer
     makes this free (§15). */</span>
  <span class="tok-kw">const</span> program = <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">getProgram</span>(programId);
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> &lt;header&gt;{program.name} …&lt;/header&gt;;
}</pre>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>
  <p>
    Both <code>ProgramHeader</code> and <code>ProgramStats</code> call
    <code>getProgram(id)</code>; only one real request happens, because the data layer is deduped (<a
      href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a
    > covers the mechanics:
    <code>cache()</code>, fetch dedupe, SWR keys). The page went from a data router back to what it
    should be: structure. And each child is now <strong>portable</strong>: drop it into any page,
    any parallel route, any dialog, hand it an id, and it works.
  </p>

  <Callout kind='info' title='This works on both sides of the boundary'>
    <p>
      Server-side, independence is free via <code>React.cache()</code> and fetch dedupe. Client-side,
      the same pattern holds with <code>useSWR</code>/React Query: two components calling <code
        >useProgram(id)</code
      > share one cache entry and one request. What you should <em>not</em> do is hand-roll it: a fetch
      in an effect has no dedupe layer, so drilling becomes "necessary" again. The anti-patterns feed
      each other.
    </p>
  </Callout>

  <p>
    One instinct still fights this: the components don't need the <em>same</em> data, so sharing one query
    feels wasteful. Picture the page-level header and the page-level content area of a program page. The
    header needs <code>name</code> and <code>status</code>; the content area needs
    <code>description</code> and <code>status</code>. Overlapping, but not identical, and the old
    instinct says either drill one fetch down from the page or write two narrow queries so nobody
    over-fetches. Resist both.
    <Chip k='next' />
    <Chip k='very' /> When the difference between the fields is not meaningfully more expensive to query
    (and for columns on the same row, it almost never is), both components should call
    <strong>the same canonical query</strong>, redundant fields and all. Dedupe collapses the two
    calls into one execution, so the page pays for one query either way; the redundancy costs
    nothing, and what it buys is the decoupling: each region asks for itself, neither knows the
    other exists, and no wire or bespoke per-component query has to be maintained.
  </p>

