  classDef blue fill:#eaf3fe,stroke:#0070f3,color:#0757ba
  classDef green fill:#e7f5ee,stroke:#2ea06d,color:#157a4c
  classDef amber fill:#fdf3e3,stroke:#b45309,color:#8a5a00
</pre>

    <Fragment slot='caption'>
      Two regions of the same page, needing overlapping-but-different fields of the same program.
      Each calls the one canonical <code>getProgram(42)</code> and reads the fields it cares about; the
      cache collapses the two calls into a single execution, so the database sees one query either way.
      Writing two narrow queries (or drilling one fetch down from the page) would save nothing measurable
      and cost the independence: with the shared query, either region can move, stream, or disappear without
      the other noticing. The same logic holds client-side with a shared SWR key (<a
        href='15-dedupe-caching.html#client'>§15.3</a
      >).
    </Fragment>
  </Diagram>

  <p>
    The same two regions also split cleanly along <em>route</em> lines. If the details region should change
    with nested sub-views (activity, history, settings) while the header stays put, make the regions <strong
      >parallel-route slots</strong
    > (<a href='13-blocking-requests.html#parallel-routes'>§13.3</a>): the layout renders <code
      >@header</code
    > and <code>@details</code> side by side, and
    <code>@details</code> carries its own sub-routes, so a navigation swaps only that slot. The dedupe
    holds across the split: both slots render in the same server request, so their <code
      >getProgram(42)</code
    > calls still collapse into one execution.
  </p>

  <Snippet>
    <pre
      class='code v-good'
      is:raw><span class="filename">app/programs/[programId]/ - the regions become slots; only @details navigates</span>[programId]/
├── layout.tsx             <span class="tok-com">renders {header} + {details}</span>
├── @header/
│   ├── page.tsx           <span class="tok-com">getProgram(42): name · status</span>
│   └── default.tsx
└── @details/
    ├── page.tsx           <span class="tok-com">getProgram(42): description · status</span>
    ├── activity/page.tsx  <span class="tok-com">/programs/[id]/activity: only this slot changes</span>
    └── default.tsx</pre>
    <CodeCaption>
      Navigating to a nested sub-view re-renders the <code>@details</code> subtree alone; the header slot
      keeps its state on soft navigations (<a href='13-blocking-requests.html#slot-subroutes'
        >§13.3</a
      >) and falls back to its <code>default.tsx</code> on hard loads. Each slot still asks the canonical
      question for itself, and the request-scoped cache still answers it once.
    </CodeCaption>
  </Snippet>

  <h2 id='streaming'>14.3 Independence changes how the page streams</h2>
  <p>
    Wiring isn't just a maintenance cost; it determines where your Suspense boundaries
    <em>can go</em>. A boundary must sit above whatever awaits. If the parent fetches and drills,
    the boundary is forced above the parent: the whole subtree is one all-or-nothing unit, gated by
    its slowest request. If children fetch for themselves, each child gets its own boundary inside
    the parent: the structure renders instantly and each region streams in as its answer arrives.
  </p>

