  <h2 id='callbacks'>14.4 Callbacks up are wires too</h2>
  <p>
    Passing data down is half the coupling story; the other half is children reporting back up. A
    callback prop that exists so a child can tell its parent "something changed, please
    refetch/re-render" is a wire pointing the other direction, and it usually means the coordination
    is happening at the wrong layer.
    <strong>Communicate through shared infrastructure instead of through parents</strong>:
    <Chip k='react' />
    <Chip k='critical' />
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>The URL</strong>: filters, tabs, selection, pagination. A filter bar that calls <code
        >router.replace()</code
      > with new search params doesn't need to know a table exists; the table re-renders because the page's
      inputs changed (<a href='09-server-resolvable-state.html'>§9</a>).
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Cache invalidation</strong>: after a mutation,
      <code>revalidatePath</code>/<code>revalidateTag</code> on the server, or SWR/React Query invalidation
      on the client. Every component reading that data updates, with no parent orchestrating (<a
        href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a
      >; the full mutation cycle is drawn in <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#refresh'
        >§5.8</a
      >).
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    Concretely: the callback you will be tempted to add most often is
    <code>onSuccess</code>: a mutation button reporting up so someone can refetch. Here is the same
    button, wired and wireless:
  </p>

  <Compare>
    <CompareCol kind='bad' label='✕ Reports up through a wire'>
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-bad'
          is:raw><span class="filename">PublishButton.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">PublishButton</span>({
  programId,
  onSuccess,
}: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
        <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">publishProgram</span>(programId);
        <span class="tok-fn">onSuccess</span>();
      }}
    &gt;
      Publish
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          The button must be handed a wire: someone above must exist, must care, and must never
          change shape.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
    <CompareCol kind='good' label="✓ Invalidates the data's name">
      <Snippet>
        <pre
          class='code v-good'
          is:raw><span class="filename">PublishButton.tsx</span><span class="tok-dir">"use client"</span>;
<span class="tok-kw">function</span> <span class="tok-fn">PublishButton</span>({ programId }: <span class="tok-fn">Props</span>) {
  <span class="tok-kw">return</span> (
    &lt;<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>
      <span class="tok-prop">onClick</span>={() =&gt;
        <span class="tok-fn">startTransition</span>(<span class="tok-kw">async</span> () =&gt; {
          <span class="tok-com">/* The action revalidates its
             tag (§5.8); or, inside an
             SWR island, mutate the key: */</span>
          <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">publishProgram</span>(programId);
          <span class="tok-kw">await</span> <span class="tok-fn">mutate</span>(
            [<span class="tok-str">"program"</span>, programId]
          );
        })
      }
    &gt;
      Publish
    &lt;/<span class="tok-fn">Button</span>&gt;
  );
}</pre>
        <CodeCaption>
          No wire: the mutation invalidates the name the data lives under, and every reader refires
          on its own.
        </CodeCaption>
      </Snippet>
    </CompareCol>
  </Compare>

  <p>
    The <em>name</em> (a cache tag, a search param, an SWR key) is the entire contract between the leaf
    and its surroundings. This is deliberately the same
    <code>PublishButton</code> as
    <a href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#self-contained'>§5.7</a>–<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#refresh'>§5.8</a
    >: that chapter argues the pattern as <em>boundary placement</em> (the leaf is the only client component,
    so the tree stays server), this one argues it as <em>wiring</em> (the leaf holds zero references to
    its surroundings, so the tree stays decoupled). Same code, two independent reasons it's right, and
    the two failures arrive together, too.
  </p>
  <p>
    Which is worth saying explicitly: the God component (<a
      href='05-client-server-boundaries.html#god-components'>§5.9</a
    >) is the maximal version of <em>this chapter's</em> failure as much as that one's. In wire terms,
    a <code>useProgramsManager()</code> that returns nine things has nine wires into every consumer; the
    tree stops being a tree of components and becomes a distribution network for one object's shape. Every
    feature threads new props through components that don't use them, every signature change ripples to
    every consumer. And because that same hook says <code>"use client"</code> at the top, the wiring cost
    and the boundary cost compound: the structure that couples everything is the structure that client-renders
    everything.
  </p>
  <p>
    Legitimate exceptions: genuinely local, ephemeral coordination: a form field telling its form it
    changed, a listbox item telling its listbox it's highlighted. The test is whether the two
    components form <em>one</em> composite widget with one responsibility, or whether a callback is smuggling
    app-level data flow through the component tree.
  </p>

  <h2 id='portability'>14.5 Portability: components you can pick up and move</h2>
  <p>
    Everything above argued wires are expensive to <em>maintain</em>. The payoff for cutting them
    has a name: <strong>portability</strong>. A component whose data arrives through props can never
    leave its page without the wires coming along: whoever hosts it next must fetch the same data,
    hold the same state, and pass the same callbacks, so "move the stats card to the dashboard"
    becomes a plumbing project. A component that addresses its data by name (a fetch keyed by its
    own props on the server, an SWR key on the client, a search param it reads itself) moves as <strong
      >one line of JSX</strong
    >. The dedupe layers of <a href='15-dedupe-caching.html'>§15</a> are what make this free: wherever
    the component lands, its question collapses into the cache with everyone else's.
  </p>

